<main id="main">

  <section class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title">What Downpick does</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">Four things, in order: connect, look, ask, take away.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="steps">
    <div class="step-pair">
      <div class="panel">
        <p class="step-eyebrow">01 · Connect</p>
        <h2 class="step-title">Every server, open at once</h2>
        <p class="step-body">Four engines — PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle, MongoDB — and as many of each as you like. Add a connection once and it stays in the sidebar, staging next to production next to the local copy, each with a dot telling you whether it's live. Passwords go into the vault, not into a config file. If a database appears while you're connected, reload the list from the connection's menu without dropping the connection.</p>
        <div class="mock">
          <div class="mock-line">⬡ Ridgeline Analytics (staging) <span class="mock-dim">·</span> <span class="mock-dim">idle</span></div>
          <div class="mock-line mock-line--strong">⬡ Ridgeline Coffee (local) <span class="mock-dim">·</span> <span class="mock-ok">connected</span></div>
          <div class="mock-line">⬡ Telemetry <span class="mock-dim">·</span> <span class="mock-dim">idle</span></div>
          <div class="mock-line">⬡ Warehouse (Oracle) <span class="mock-dim">·</span> <span class="mock-dim">idle</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="panel">
        <p class="step-eyebrow">02 · Look</p>
        <h2 class="step-title">See the shape of your data</h2>
        <p class="step-body">The explorer goes all the way down — databases, schemas, tables, columns and their types. On MongoDB, collections and their fields. On Oracle, your own schemas rather than Oracle's: <span class="mono">SYS</span>, <span class="mono">SYSTEM</span> and the rest stay out of the tree. Double-click any table to query it.</p>
        <div class="mock">
          <div class="mock-line mock-line--strong">▾ ▦ coffees</div>
          <div class="mock-line mock-split"><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;— name</span><span class="mock-dim">text</span></div>
          <div class="mock-line mock-split"><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;— price_cents</span><span class="mock-dim">integer</span></div>
          <div class="mock-line mock-split"><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;— attributes</span><span class="mock-dim">jsonb</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="steps steps--tight">
    <div class="panel step-split">
      <div>
        <p class="step-eyebrow">03 · Ask</p>
        <h2 class="step-title">Write it, or describe it</h2>
        <p class="step-body step-body--mid">The editor highlights and autocompletes SQL and MongoDB shell syntax, formats on a keystroke, and runs the selection if you've selected something — or just the statement under the cursor, on <span class="mono">F9</span>.</p>
        <p class="step-body step-body--last">Or press <span class="accent-inline">✦ Ask AI</span> and say what you're after. It reads your table and column names, writes the query, and waits. It reads the query you've already written too, so "what does this do?" and "group this by month" work on what's in front of you — select a fragment and that fragment is all it sees, and all it replaces. Running anything is still your call, and a DELETE with no WHERE clause still asks twice. Every conversation is saved, so the question you asked last week is one click away in the history.</p>
      </div>
      <img src="/assets/ai.gif" alt="Ask AI reading the schema and writing a query into the editor"
             width="1440" height="805" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-fallback="Ask AI demo">
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="steps steps--tight">
    <div class="panel">
      <p class="step-eyebrow">04 · Take away</p>
      <div class="step-split step-split--top">
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-title">Results that behave</h2>
          <p class="step-body step-body--last">A grid that scrolls smoothly at a hundred thousand rows, with types under every column heading. Mongo documents flatten into a table, or open as a tree, one document at a time. Then copy it as a table into Slack or Sheets, or export CSV or Excel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="result-side">
          <div class="stat-row">
            <div class="stat">
              <div class="stat-n">1,260</div>
              <div class="stat-l">rows returned</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat">
              <div class="stat-n">44ms</div>
              <div class="stat-l">round trip</div>
            </div>
            <div class="stat">
              <div class="stat-n">3</div>
              <div class="stat-l">export formats</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="chip-row">
            <span class="chip">⧉ Copy</span>
            <span class="chip">↓ CSV</span>
            <span class="chip">↓ Excel</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="eyebrow">And the rest</h2>
    <div class="rest-grid">
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Oracle, with nothing to install</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">No Instant Client, no ODBC or JDBC driver, no ORACLE_HOME. Downpick speaks to Oracle 12.1 and later by itself — it asks for a service name, because Oracle is the one engine whose databases can't be listed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">An encrypted vault, wherever you want it</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Every connection password is encrypted behind one master password, locked on launch and re-locked after a period of inactivity you choose. Keep the file where it lands or put it on an encrypted volume — the first launch offers the choice, and Settings can move it later.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Cancel and timeouts</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">A running query shows how long it has been going and a button to stop it. Cancel with a keystroke, or set a timeout — 30 seconds by default — that stops it for you.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Confirmation on destructive statements</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">UPDATE or DELETE with no WHERE clause, and Mongo updateMany or deleteMany with an empty filter, ask before they run.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Tabs, restored</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Your open query tabs, pane sizes, and window position come back exactly as you left them.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Errors, verbatim</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">The server's own message, in monospace, with the line number — not a friendlier rewrite of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Keyboard first</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Run, cancel, format, new connection, lock the vault, open settings — all on shortcuts, all on the menu bar too.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Your choice of AI provider</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, Azure, or a local model on your own machine. Keys live in the vault.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Nothing phones home</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">No telemetry, no account, no network access from the window that renders your data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">A Messages view</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Statements that return no rows — UPDATE, DELETE, the DDL half of a batch — get their own list, one line each, with the rows affected and the time taken.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">Ask AI history</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Conversations are saved, one per tab, and the history lists them all across every connection. Resume one, or clear them all at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">A nudge when it finishes</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Start something slow and go elsewhere. Downpick raises a system notification when the window is in the background, an in-app one when it isn't, and nothing at all for a query that came back before the threshold you set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">One statement out of many</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Put the cursor inside a statement in a longer script and press F9 to run only that one. It's on the Query menu, next to Run and Cancel.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rest">
        <h3 class="rest-title">No transaction control, yet</h3>
        <p class="rest-body">Worth saying plainly: statements commit as they run, on every engine. A script that fails halfway leaves the statements before it committed. The confirmation on an unqualified UPDATE or DELETE is the safety net until that changes.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="cta">
      <h2 class="cta-title">Try it on your own database.</h2>
      <p class="cta-body">Nothing to sign up for. Download, set a password, connect.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a class="btn btn--accent btn--lg" href="/download.html">Download Downpick {{ release.version }}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
